UP|52|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Well done. Let us see how they do in 3@D, which is where they will land if East bids 2N (takeout for the minors). They will take 3 diamonds on length, plus a ruff, 2 spades since the SA is on side, and 2 hearts at least for 8 tricks. Furthermore, because the @CJ comes down, they might end up with a club trick.|
PG| ***** 38 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|So 3@D likely either down 1 or making and is a good bid particularly in Match Points. In general, if the points are evenly divided and the opponents end up at the 2 level in an 8+ card fit, you are not destined for a good board.|
PG| ***** 39 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|East has a weak hand but given the auction East knows partner has to have a pretty good hand. Therefore, 5 HCP it is not that weak. When you balance, which is what 2N is, you are bidding both your hand and partner's hand. So 2N is a conceivable bid although far from obvious.|
PG| ***** 40 ***** |


