qx|A5|st||rh||ah|Board 5 IMPs   -   January 31, 2024|pn|hondo717,mcannon,ashhoki,Rustgi65|
md|3S74HKT85DJ53CQT96,SAKT85HA9DQ876C84,S2H743DK94CAK7532,SQJ963HQJ62DAT2CJ|sv|n|
mb|P|mb|1S|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|I don't really agree with opening 1@S. East has 11 HCP and 9 cards in the majors so supposedly meets the |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=ruleof20,Rule of 20^*N|CP||FP||at| if you just use raw HCP to make the calculation. |
PG| ***** 91 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|The hand should be downgraded because of its many quacks (queens and jacks), which are not worth as much as their HCP indicate. Moreover, the @CJ needs to be discounted but I will let the 1@S bid stand.|
PG| ***** 92 ***** |


mb|P|mb|?|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|lb|*bWhat should West bid here?^^2n|mb|2N|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|When partner opens 1 of a major and you have 4+ cards in that major you must immediately show that support. If you don't you are denying 4 card support. With an opening hand and 5 spades, the West hand has a clear 2N bid, showing 4 spades and a game force.|
PG| ***** 94 ***** |


mb|3C|mb|?|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|lb|*bWhat should East bid here?^^D|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|East must now respond correctly to 2N. Absent interference, East must bid 3@C over 2N to show a singleton or void in clubs since North did bid 3@C. Therefore, East should double as ^*BStolen Bid^*n meaning East intended to bid 3@C had North passed.|
PG| ***** 96 ***** |


mb|D|mb|P|mb|?|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|lb|*bWhat should West bid here?^^3H|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|West has mixed feelings about East being short in clubs. It means they have no wasted club values. Furthermore, West has a fairly good hand. West has 13 HCP, 1 length point, 2 points for the 2 doubletons, and 1 point for the quality spade suit for 17 |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=dummypoints,Dummy Points^*N|CP||FP||at|. I would bid 3@H, to show a heart control and deny a diamond control.|
PG| ***** 98 ***** |


mb|3H|mb|P|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|?|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|East must now bid 4@D to show a diamond control.|
PG| ***** 99 ***** |


mb|4D|mb|P|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|4N|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Now knowing all suits are controlled West can bid 4N RKCB and bid 6 if their side has 4 key cards. With 5 spades there is no need to worry about the @SQ since the 10 card fit means a 2-1 split is very likely and the @SQ is not a loser even if the defenders hold it.|
PG| ***** 100 ***** |


mb|P|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|5D|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|I prefer 0314 responses. So East bids 5@D to show 1 (or rarely 4) keycards.|
PG| ***** 101 ***** |


mb|P|mb|6S|mb|P|mb|P|mb|P|
pg| ***** 102 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|
pc|C6|pc|C4|pc|CK|pc|CJ|
pg| ***** 103 ***** |


pc|D9|pc|DA|pc|D3|pc|D6|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|This was both a misdefense by North and a misplay by East. North should not lead a diamond, because it is likely to give away a trick. East must have the @DA, since they showed 1 key card, and the 4 other key cards are visible to North (3 in dummy plus the @CA). And East should duck the diamond, because playing the @DA guarantees losing a diamond trick for down 1.|
